+# CLI Contract: `tarx create`
+
+**Feature**: `002-create-archive-command` | **Date**: 2026-11-12 | **Spec**: [spec.md](../spec.md)
+
+This document is the authoritative, testable description of the `create` command. It **extends**
+[`specs/001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md`](../../001-tarx-cli/contracts/cli.md), which remains
+authoritative and unchanged for `list` and `extract`: every statement in it still holds
+word-for-word after `create` is added (FR-101). Anything not permitted here is a defect.
+
+Message texts below are the shapes the implementation must produce; tests assert on the stable,
+operator-relevant parts (exit status, which stream, the named path or member, the key phrase)
+rather than on incidental wording.
+
+---
+
+## Invocation
+
+```text
+python -m tarx list    ARCHIVE
+python -m tarx extract ARCHIVE DEST    [--overwrite]
+python -m tarx create  ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]
+```
+
+Requires `src/` on `PYTHONPATH`; there is no installation step (MC-101).
+
+- `ARCHIVE` — path of the archive to write. Must be non-empty. Must not already exist, unless
+  `--overwrite` is given and what exists is a regular file.
+- `PATH...` — one or more relative paths, each naming a regular file or a directory. Must be
+  non-empty strings.
+- `--overwrite` — accepted by `create` and by `extract`; never by `list`.
+
+A `PATH` beginning with `-` is parsed as an option and is a usage error; `--` ends option
+parsing, so `create out.tar -- -odd-name` works. This is conventional `argparse` behaviour and
+is not special-cased.
+
+---
+
+## Exit statuses
+
+Unchanged from 001: exactly three statuses are producible across the whole tool (FR-121).
+
+| Status | Meaning for `create` |
+|---|---|
+| `0` | The archive was created and the success line was written |
+| `2` | Any failure other than an archive-exists refusal: usage error, rejected PATH, missing/unreadable PATH, refused member, duplicate member, unusable archive path, exceeded bound, file changed size mid-read, closed stdout |
+| `3` | `create` was refused **solely** because a regular file already exists at `ARCHIVE` and `--overwrite` was not given |
+
+---
+
+## Stream discipline (FR-112)
+
+- **stdout** carries exactly one line on success and nothing on failure.
+- **stderr** carries every error and diagnostic message, one line, newline-terminated.
+- A failing `create` never prints a success line.
+- No stack trace reaches stderr for any failure described here (SR-111).
+
+---
+
+## `create ARCHIVE PATH... [--overwrite]`
+
+### Success (exit 0)
+
+Writes a tar archive at `ARCHIVE` containing every member derived from the `PATH`s, then prints
+exactly one line:
+
+```text
+created ARCHIVE with N files
+```
+
+`ARCHIVE` is echoed **as the user wrote it**, passed through the SR-110 escaping first. `N`
+counts **regular files only**; directory members are excluded. There is no pluralisation:
+`created out.tar with 0 files` and `created out.tar with 1 files` are both correct (FR-110,
+FR-111).
+
+```console
+$ python -m tarx create photos.tar.gz photos
+created photos.tar.gz with 2 files
+$ echo $?
+0
+```
+
+### Member names (FR-104)
+
+| Given | Members recorded |
+|---|---|
+| `notes.txt` (a file) | `notes.txt` |
+| `photos` (a directory holding `a.jpg`, `b.jpg`) | `photos`, `photos/a.jpg`, `photos/b.jpg` |
+| `./photos/` | identical to `photos` — redundant `.` components and trailing separators are normalised away |
+| `photos/sub` (a directory) | `photos/sub`, then its contents |
+
+Every directory encountered is recorded as a directory member, which is what lets an empty
+directory survive a `create` → `extract` round trip (FR-105). Ancestor directories *above* a
+PATH are never added: `create out.tar photos/sub` records `photos/sub`, not `photos`.
+
+Directory member names are recorded without a trailing slash; a reader cannot distinguish the
+two forms, because `tarfile` strips a trailing slash from a directory member's name on read.
+
+### Member order (FR-106)
+
+1. `PATH`s in the order given on the command line.
+2. Within a directory: the directory's own member first, then its entries in ascending
+   code-point order of name, with each subdirectory's contents immediately following that
+   subdirectory's member (pre-order depth-first).
+
+Running `create` twice over an unchanged tree produces the same member names in the same order —
+and, because timestamps are fixed, byte-identical archives.
+
+### Compression (FR-108)
+
+| `ARCHIVE` ends in | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `.tar.gz` or `.tgz`, any letter case | gzip-compressed tar |
+| anything else | plain, uncompressed tar |
+
+This is the one place in the tool where the filename decides a format. `list` and `extract`
+continue to decide from content (001 FR-008), so a deliberately misnamed archive still reads
+correctly.
+
+### Recorded member properties (SR-108)
+
+| Field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| member type | regular file or directory only — never a link or special file |
+| `mode` | `0o644` (file), `0o755` (directory); setuid, setgid, sticky never set |
+| `uid` / `gid` | `0` / `0` |
+| `uname` / `gname` | empty / empty |
+| `mtime` | `0` |
+| contents | byte-for-byte as read from disk (FR-107) |
+
+The on-disk owner, group, permissions, and timestamps are never recorded. The gzip container,
+when used, records no filename and a zero timestamp.
+
+### Archive file properties (SR-107)
+
+Created with mode `0o600` — owner read and write — set **at creation**, never by a later
+`chmod`, and never with setuid, setgid, or sticky bits. The temporary file used on the way to
+the final archive is created with the same permissions.
+
+### Validate-before-write guarantee (FR-118, FR-119)
+
+Every `PATH` is validated, the whole member set is determined, and the archive path is
+classified **before** any archive content is written. Content is then written to a temporary
+file in `ARCHIVE`'s own directory and renamed into place as the last step.
+
+Therefore, on **every** failure, at **any** point:
+
+- nothing exists at `ARCHIVE` if nothing was there before;
+- a pre-existing regular file at `ARCHIVE` is byte-for-byte unchanged;
+- no temporary file remains anywhere;
+- nothing outside `ARCHIVE` is created, modified, or deleted — every `PATH` is read only
+  (FR-122).
+
+Unlike `extract`, `create` has **no** documented partial-output exception. Compare 001's
+contract case 9.
+
+### Round-trip guarantee (FR-109)
+
+Archives produced by `create` are readable by this tool's own `list` and `extract`: `list`
+prints every member name, `extract` reproduces the tree with identical relative paths and
+byte-identical contents, and no member is rejected by any requirement of the 001 spec. The
+producer enforces this by passing every computed member name through the *same* validator
+`extract` applies, and by sharing the *same* resource-bound constants.
+
+### Failures
+
+| # | Condition | Status | Message names |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | No `ARCHIVE`, no `PATH`, unknown option, empty-string `ARCHIVE` or `PATH` | 2 | usage (FR-102, SR-112) |
+| 2 | A `PATH` is absolute, contains `..`, is `.` or `..`, or has a drive-letter/UNC prefix | 2 | the `PATH` as given (SR-101) |
+| 3 | A `PATH` does not exist — including when other `PATH`s do | 2 | that `PATH` (FR-117) |
+| 4 | A `PATH`, or an entry beneath one, is a symlink, hard link, device, FIFO, or socket | 2 | the member **and its kind** (SR-102) |
+| 5 | A `PATH` or a directory beneath one cannot be read | 2 | that path (spec Edge Cases) |
+| 6 | Two members would carry the same name (repeated, nested, or equivalently spelled `PATH`s) | 2 | the duplicated member (SR-104) |
+| 7 | `ARCHIVE` is named directly as a `PATH` | 2 | the archive path (SR-109) |
+| 8 | A regular file exists at `ARCHIVE`, no `--overwrite` | **3** | the archive path (FR-114) |
+| 9 | `ARCHIVE` exists as a directory, symlink, FIFO, device, or socket — with or without `--overwrite` | 2 | the archive path and what it is (FR-116) |
+| 10 | `ARCHIVE`'s parent directory does not exist or is not a directory | 2 | the archive path (FR-116) |
+| 11 | Member count, single-file size, or total size exceeds a bound | 2 | the bound and the member (SR-105) |
+| 12 | A file changes size while it is being read | 2 | the member (FR-120) |
+| 13 | The archive cannot be written (disk full, unwritable directory) | 2 | the archive path (FR-113) |
+| 14 | stdout closed before the success line is written | 2 | — (the archive is complete) |
+
+In cases 1–13 no archive is created and no pre-existing archive is modified. Case 14 is the one
+case where a non-zero status accompanies a successfully created archive; nothing is printed to
+stdout and no crash report reaches stderr.
+
+`--overwrite` changes **only** case 8. It does not suppress any other case.
+
+A symbolic link inside a directory `PATH` makes the whole command fail (case 4) — it is never
+skipped and never followed. A directory containing any symbolic link therefore cannot be
+archived by this tool, which is the mirror image of the restriction `extract` already imposes.
+
+---
+
+## Usage errors (FR-102, SR-112)
+
+All of the following write a usage message to **stderr** and exit **2**:
+
+- `create` with no `ARCHIVE`
+- `create ARCHIVE` with no `PATH`
+- an unrecognised option (`create --verbose out.tar p`)
+- an empty-string `ARCHIVE` or `PATH`
+- `--overwrite` given to `list`
+
+`--help` / `-h` remains conventional `argparse` behaviour: usage text on stdout, exit 0.
+
+Argument values are used as filesystem paths directly. No subprocess is spawned and no shell is
+invoked anywhere in this tool, so there is no command-string interpolation path (SR-112).
+
+---
+
+## Name escaping on output (SR-110)
+
+Identical to 001's SR-007 rules and performed by the same `escape_name` function: C0 controls,
+`DEL`, C1 controls, and Unicode categories `Cc`, `Cf`, `Cs`, `Co`, `Cn` are escaped; short forms
+`\n`, `\r`, `\t`, `\\` are used where they apply; everything else — including all printable
+non-ASCII — is emitted unchanged. This applies to the archive path on the success line as well
+as to every path and member in a diagnostic, so a crafted name can neither inject terminal
+control sequences nor forge an extra output line.
+
+---
+
+## Resource bounds (SR-105)
+
+| Bound | Value |
+|---|---|
+| members per archive | 10,000 |
+| total bytes across all members | 1 GiB |
+| bytes in any single member | 1 GiB |
+
+The same fixed constants `list` and `extract` enforce, read from the same module. Checked
+against stat sizes while the member set is built, and against bytes actually read while the
+archive is written. Exceeding a bound is always an error (exit 2) and never a truncated archive.
+
+---
+
+## Invariants a conforming implementation must uphold
+
+1. `list` and `extract` behave exactly as 001's contract describes, in every respect (FR-101).
+2. Only statuses 0, 2, 3 are ever returned by any command (FR-121).
+3. stdout is byte-exact: `created ARCHIVE with N files\n` on success, empty on failure (FR-112).
+4. A non-zero status is never accompanied by a success line (FR-112).
+5. No traceback reaches stderr for any failure listed here (SR-111).
+6. A failed `create` leaves the archive path exactly as it was, and leaves no temporary file
+   anywhere (FR-119, SC-106).
+7. Nothing outside `ARCHIVE` is created, modified, or deleted; `PATH`s are read only (FR-122).
+8. No link or special file is ever recorded as a member (SR-102).
+9. No member's contents are ever held whole in memory (SR-106).
+10. No member discloses the local account's user name, group name, uid, or gid (SR-108).
+11. Every archive `create` produces is accepted by this tool's own `list` and `extract` (FR-109).
